  "output": "Now I have a complete picture. This codebase is unusually well-engineered for performance already — the data layer comments explicitly reason about query costs. Here's what I actually found, not manufactured issues:\n\n## Summary\n\nThis is a Server-Components-first Next.js app with a thin, deliberately-scoped client layer (one polling widget, one interactive queue). The backend query layer is already disciplined about batching. The real opportunities are concentrated in the one client-heavy surface: the conflict review queue.\n\n---\n\n## 1. N+1 query patterns — none found\n\nEvery list-plus-detail screen already batches correctly:\n\n- `getQueuePage` (`lib/server/conflicts.ts:172-200`) runs rows/counts/total via `Promise.all`, then does **one** grouped `countChildrenByParent` query for the whole page instead of a per-row lookup — the comment even calls this out (\"Three round trips, not three hundred\").\n- `getAuditPage` and `getExportSizing` use the same `Promise.all` batching pattern.\n- `countChildrenByParent` (`lib/server/entities.ts:28-51`) groups by `(sourceId, parentExternalId)` in one query and returns a `Map`, rather than querying per entity.\n\nNo action needed here — this is the pattern to keep, not fix.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Unnecessary re-renders — real issue in the conflict queue\n\n`ConflictQueue` (`components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx`) renders up to **100 rows** (`QUEUE_PAGE_SIZE = 100`) in `ConflictDenseTable`, `ConflictFocus`, or the ledger view. Pressing `j`/`k` only changes `selectedIndex` in the parent, but none of the row components are memoized, so **all 100 rows re-render on every keystroke**, each recomputing `diffSegments` (`lib/diff-chars.ts`) and its own class-name logic.\n\nIt's compounded by closures recreated per row on every render:\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-queue.tsx:172-187 — ledger view\n{rows.map((row, index) => (\n  <ConflictDiffRow\n    key={row.conflictId}\n    row={row}\n    selected={index === clamped}\n    onSelect={() => setSelectedIndex(index)}        // new fn every render\n    submit={submitFor(row.conflictId)}               // new fn every render\n    ...\n  />\n))}\n```\n\nEven wrapping the row in `React.memo` wouldn't help today because `onSelect`/`submit` break referential equality every render. Fix both together:\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-diff-row.tsx\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = React.memo(function ConflictDiffRow({ ... }) {\n  ...\n});\n```\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-queue.tsx — stabilize the per-row callbacks\nconst selectRow = useCallback((index: number) => setSelectedIndex(index), []);\n\n// pass index instead of a per-row closure\n<ConflictDiffRow\n  key={row.conflictId}\n  row={row}\n  selected={index === clamped}\n  onSelect={selectRow}          // stable\n  index={index}\n  submit={submitFor(row.conflictId)}   // memoize this map too, see below\n/>\n```\n\n`submitFor` itself is stable (`useCallback` on `submit`), but calling `submitFor(row.conflictId)` inline still allocates a new closure per row per render. Precompute once with `useMemo`:\n\n```tsx\nconst submitByRow = useMemo(\n  () => new Map(rows.map((r) => [r.conflictId, submitFor(r.conflictId)])),\n  [rows, submitFor],\n);\n```\n\nSame pattern applies to `ConflictDenseTable` and `ConflictFocus`'s `ListRow` — neither is memoized, and `ListRow` recomputes `cn(...)` class strings for all 100 rows on every selection change even though only two rows' visual state actually changed.\n\nThis matters most in the dense view (36px rows, up to 100 on screen) where the keyboard-only workflow (`AGENTS.md`'s stated goal — \"never touch the mouse\") is exactly the workflow that fires this re-render on every navigation keystroke.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Caching opportunities — mostly already correctly *not* cached\n\nThe console is explicitly designed to always read fresh Postgres state (`await connection()` opts every list/detail query out of static rendering) because this is a review tool where stale conflict/audit data is a correctness problem, not a UX nicety. Adding `unstable_cache` here would be a bug, not an optimization — I'd leave this alone.\n\nOne legitimate spot: `RunLive`'s TanStack Query client sets `staleTime: 0` (`components/query-provider.tsx:26`), which is correct for a 2-second poll, but note `refetchInterval` already handles freshness — `staleTime: 0` plus polling is fine, no change needed.\n\nIf you later add a second polling surface, keep `QueryProvider` scoped to that leaf like it is now (deliberately not mounted at the root, per that file's comment) rather than centralizing client-side fetching.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Memory leaks — none found\n\nChecked every `useEffect`/`setInterval`/`addEventListener`:\n\n- `Elapsed`'s `setInterval` is cleaned up (`run-live.tsx:106-107`).\n- `ConflictQueue`'s `keydown` listener is removed on unmount (`conflict-queue.tsx:130-131`).\n- `ListRow`'s `scrollIntoView` effect has no subscription to leak.\n- `QueryClient` is created once via `useState(() => new QueryClient(...))` and scoped to `RunLive`'s subtree, not the root — it's garbage-collected with the component.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Redundant computations\n\n**Real, minor:** `Side` in `components/conflicts/conflict-sides.tsx:94-96` calls `provenance(side)` twice per render (once to check truthiness, once to render):\n\n```tsx\n// before\n{provenance(side) ? (\n  <p className=\"text-body text-g-500\">{provenance(side)}</p>\n) : null}\n\n// after\n{(() => { const info = provenance(side); return info ? <p className=\"text-body text-g-500\">{info}</p> : null; })()}\n```\nor simpler, hoist it above the JSX:\n```tsx\nconst info = provenance(side);\n...\n{info ? <p className=\"text-body text-g-500\">{info}</p> : null}\n```\n\n**Same root cause as #2:** `diffSegments` (O(n) char scan, `lib/diff-chars.ts`) runs fresh in every `DiffValue` instance on every re-render because nothing upstream is memoized — up to 200+ calls (2 sides × up to 100 rows) per keystroke in the dense/ledger views. Fixing the memoization in #2 eliminates this redundant work as a side effect; no separate change needed if you memo the row components.\n\n---\n\n**Net takeaway:** the server/data layer needs nothing — it's already built the way most teams end up after a perf incident. The one concrete, worthwhile change is memoizing the conflict-queue row components (`ConflictDiffRow`, `ConflictDenseTable`'s row rendering, `ConflictFocus`'s `ListRow`) and stabilizing their callback props, since that's the one place with a 100-item list re-rendering on every keyboard interaction.\n",
